The book is thinking in bets by Annie Duke.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:48.220
Annie Duke is both a poker player and a sort
of business consultant.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:54.480
And thinking in bets is a very interesting
book for me as someone in politics because

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:01.020
we deal very often with beliefs and so often
conversations in politics are this is true

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.930
or that's not true.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:07.399
And in reality, even when we say those things,
typically what we're doing is we're, we're

00:01:07.399 --> 00:01:09.829
assessing the probability that something is
true.

00:01:09.829 --> 00:01:14.859
We have some level of confidence that something
is true that is greater than zero but less

00:01:14.859 --> 00:01:20.630
than 100 and what Annie Duke very interestingly
does and in a way that with a lot of interesting

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:27.749
stories and anecdotes and examples is she
says we would benefit from thinking in bets

00:01:27.749 --> 00:01:31.549
when we think about our views and decisions
that we are making.

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:36.899
So much like I said in the last segment, if
you told me I need to make a bet about is

00:01:36.899 --> 00:01:42.539
Michael Bloomberg or Amy Klobuchar more likely
to be the nominee, there's a certain amount

00:01:42.539 --> 00:01:45.869
money that I'd be willing to bet based on
what the return would be.

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:49.819
And this is where the idea of, you know, even
money, odds and bedding comes from when you

00:01:49.819 --> 00:01:57.549
have odds, you might bet on something that
is very unlikely if the reward feels in proportion

00:01:57.549 --> 00:01:59.729
to how unlikely it is.

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:06.630
So the book talks a lot about, you know, when
we say, uh, clearly the, um, uh, clearly the,

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:12.380
the, uh, ice caps melting are a sign of climate
change.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:16.810
We are really dealing in an assessment of
how likely we believe that to be.

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:22.410
And when you say to me, are you 100% sure
about this, that or the other thing about

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:23.550
climate change?

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:28.690
No, not a hundred percent sure, but I'm sure
enough that I wouldn't bet against it.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:33.810
And that is exactly the framework that Annie
Duke talks about and says in conversation

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:40.490
we would also be much more open to other ideas
if the conversations are less about, I believe

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:45.930
a you believe the opposite of a and more about,
well I believe this would 70% probability,

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.570
you seem to believe it only with 40% probability.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:53.110
Let's figure out where the difference comes
in and let's figure out what level of risk

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:59.320
or what level of loss we are willing to accept
very important political applications to the

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:01.020
idea of thinking in bets.

